but we did give them some feedback.

527
00:22:14,430 --> 00:22:15,263
Yeah.

528
00:22:16,530 --> 00:22:18,090
- So, for Dreamscape Learn,

529
00:22:18,090 --> 00:22:20,520
it was built in collaboration with faculty

530
00:22:20,520 --> 00:22:22,350
at Arizona State University.

531
00:22:22,350 --> 00:22:24,510
So faculty were directly involved in that.

532
00:22:24,510 --> 00:22:27,600
Now, albeit it wasn't our faculty at that stage

533
00:22:27,600 --> 00:22:30,450
when they initially developed, but so far,

534
00:22:30,450 --> 00:22:33,690
Dreamscape Learn has been really receptive to feedback.

535
00:22:33,690 --> 00:22:35,850
When I was at Merced College,

536
00:22:35,850 --> 00:22:39,810
we had about 25 faculty attend either headquarters at LA

537
00:22:39,810 --> 00:22:41,490
or go to Arizona State University

538
00:22:41,490 --> 00:22:43,740
and provided critical feedback,

539
00:22:43,740 --> 00:22:45,300
things that they wanted to see in the product,

540
00:22:45,300 --> 00:22:46,740
the roadmap for the product.

541
00:22:46,740 --> 00:22:47,940
And of course, Dreamscape Learn

542
00:22:47,940 --> 00:22:49,740
was really receptive to that feedback

543
00:22:49,740 --> 00:22:52,620
because they're trying to advertise to faculty

544
00:22:52,620 --> 00:22:54,720
and support faculty in this mission.

545
00:22:54,720 --> 00:22:56,705
So really, I think the early adoption

546
00:22:56,705 --> 00:22:58,320
of Arizona State University

547
00:22:58,320 --> 00:23:00,130
partnering with Dreamscape Immersive

548
00:23:01,080 --> 00:23:03,330
provided a lot of feedback and scope

549
00:23:03,330 --> 00:23:05,310
for what they want to do in the future.

550
00:23:05,310 --> 00:23:08,910
Also, individual colleges continue to take those visits

551
00:23:08,910 --> 00:23:10,200
and provide feedback.

552
00:23:10,200 --> 00:23:12,300
That's part of the roadmap of Dreamscape Learn

553
00:23:12,300 --> 00:23:13,980
is to have that continual loop.

554
00:23:13,980 --> 00:23:16,170
And for us, as the California Community College System,

555
00:23:16,170 --> 00:23:18,300
this community of practice pilot project

556
00:23:18,300 --> 00:23:21,060
that we're working on, we hope, like I said,

557
00:23:21,060 --> 00:23:24,210
to have 75 faculty provide critical feedback

558
00:23:24,210 --> 00:23:26,610
by the end of this pilot over the next three years.

559
00:23:26,610 --> 00:23:29,550
After those three years, we'll see where this project goes.

560
00:23:29,550 --> 00:23:31,170
But we want to maintain that.

561
00:23:31,170 --> 00:23:34,200
We are working on the evaluation processes right now

562
00:23:34,200 --> 00:23:38,490
to assess that student experiences, faculty experiences,

563
00:23:38,490 --> 00:23:40,200
Canvas administrator experiences,

564
00:23:40,200 --> 00:23:41,460
because a lot of this functions

565
00:23:41,460 --> 00:23:43,920
and runs through the Canvas LMS.

566
00:23:43,920 --> 00:23:47,070
So, we want to continue to have Faculty Voice in that,

567
00:23:47,070 --> 00:23:47,903
because at the end of the day,

568
00:23:47,903 --> 00:23:50,190
it's the faculty's decision if they want to adopt

569
00:23:50,190 --> 00:23:51,150
and use this

570
00:23:51,150 --> 00:23:53,817
in their teaching and learning practices or not.

571
00:23:53,817 --> 00:23:56,913
And so we wanna make sure we have their voices in that room.

572
00:23:58,530 --> 00:24:00,810
- And same for the NVIDIA project we had

573
00:24:00,810 --> 00:24:04,530
as part of the training that the instructors received

574
00:24:04,530 --> 00:24:06,330
over the course of a day and a half,

575
00:24:06,330 --> 00:24:09,270
they all provided feedback

576
00:24:09,270 --> 00:24:10,740
at the end of the training session.

577
00:24:10,740 --> 00:24:11,700
There was part of that

578
00:24:11,700 --> 00:24:14,070
that they needed to tell them how it worked

579
00:24:14,070 --> 00:24:17,280
or if it worked for them, et cetera, what could be better.

580
00:24:17,280 --> 00:24:19,410
And then as part of the entire process,

581
00:24:19,410 --> 00:24:21,660
there was an interview that they had to complete afterwards

582
00:24:21,660 --> 00:24:23,070
to receive the certification.

583
00:24:23,070 --> 00:24:24,540
And in that interview also,

584
00:24:24,540 --> 00:24:28,054
feedback was able to be provided by the faculty members

585
00:24:28,054 --> 00:24:30,695
to the team, to the NVIDIA team and to us,

586
00:24:30,695 --> 00:24:34,200
as they're completing their certification exam, basically.

587
00:24:34,200 --> 00:24:35,460
Interview, really.

588
00:24:35,460 --> 00:24:39,120
So it's built within it for faculty to be able to do that,

589
00:24:39,120 --> 00:24:42,000
and we're running a second cohort this fall semester,

590
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:45,000
so there'll be more of a chance for that,

591
00:24:45,000 --> 00:24:47,100
more people from across the state as well.

592
00:24:47,100 --> 00:24:48,960
We're hoping to do this one online also.

593
00:24:48,960 --> 00:24:51,390
And there's gonna be an announcements through ASCCC

594
00:24:51,390 --> 00:24:55,020
and to many other local districts, et cetera,

595
00:24:55,020 --> 00:24:57,690
so that people can sign up for that one.

596
00:24:57,690 --> 00:24:59,640
It's gonna be also a higher level one,

597
00:24:59,640 --> 00:25:01,050
it's gonna be deep learning.

598
00:25:01,050 --> 00:25:03,150
So there has to be some knowledge of AI previous to that.

599
00:25:03,150 --> 00:25:06,540
But we are also expanding to other ones in the near future

600
00:25:06,540 --> 00:25:07,623
that anyone can take.

601
00:25:10,033 --> 00:25:12,330
- So for Nectar, absolutely.

602
00:25:12,330 --> 00:25:14,790
Feedback was consistent

603
00:25:14,790 --> 00:25:18,840
and encouraged on a very regular basis.

604
00:25:18,840 --> 00:25:21,461
And through those that pilot channel,

605
00:25:21,461 --> 00:25:23,190
what kind of happened was,

606
00:25:23,190 --> 00:25:27,480
is that faculty actually influenced source limits,

607
00:25:27,480 --> 00:25:29,880
the tone settings that I was talking about earlier,

608
00:25:29,880 --> 00:25:34,350
those analytics, what the faculty wanted to see from it.

609
00:25:34,350 --> 00:25:36,720
And the most importantly for us

610
00:25:36,720 --> 00:25:40,233
was that multilingual support that we ended up getting.

611
00:25:42,360 --> 00:25:45,930
And they have ongoing monthly feedback

612
00:25:45,930 --> 00:25:49,680
where faculty that are using across the state can come in

613
00:25:49,680 --> 00:25:53,700
and take a look at what has changed with Nectar

614
00:25:53,700 --> 00:25:57,660
'cause they're continually updating, and then get feedback,

615
00:25:57,660 --> 00:26:00,870
and then get ideas for what they can improve on

616
00:26:00,870 --> 00:26:04,920
or bring to the table for the next iteration of Nectar.

617
00:26:04,920 --> 00:26:08,583
So, really good process, very great team with that.

618
00:26:10,680 --> 00:26:11,790
- Nice.

619
00:26:11,790 --> 00:26:12,930
So here is a question

620
00:26:12,930 --> 00:26:16,067
a lot of people might have an intriguing answer.

621
00:26:17,050 --> 00:26:19,289
- What would you tell your faculty colleagues

622
00:26:19,289 --> 00:26:22,044
about using AI or emerging technologies

623
00:26:22,044 --> 00:26:24,540
as part of the learning experience,

624
00:26:24,540 --> 00:26:26,520
either as part of a pilot or in general,

625
00:26:26,520 --> 00:26:29,430
if you're testing it out on your own without support

626
00:26:29,430 --> 00:26:31,710
from some sort of group.

627
00:26:31,710 --> 00:26:34,579
And maybe we'll start in the middle this time with...

628
00:26:34,579 --> 00:26:35,412
Okay.

629
00:26:37,110 --> 00:26:38,600
- So I...

630
00:26:39,690 --> 00:26:42,300
This one is, I'm a little biased.

631
00:26:42,300 --> 00:26:44,280
I'm very techy,

632
00:26:44,280 --> 00:26:47,247
and so it's easy for me to adopt something like this

633
00:26:47,247 --> 00:26:49,020
and to jump in.

634
00:26:49,020 --> 00:26:51,690
But ultimately,

635
00:26:51,690 --> 00:26:54,401
what we have to keep in mind is who our,

636
00:26:54,401 --> 00:26:57,457
let's say, customers are, who are our students?

637
00:26:58,314 --> 00:27:00,258
And our students, they're afraid.

638
00:27:00,258 --> 00:27:02,340
They're worried that they're not being trained

639
00:27:02,340 --> 00:27:04,320
on what the job will be.

640
00:27:04,320 --> 00:27:06,690
And we've seen examples of that nowadays,

641
00:27:06,690 --> 00:27:09,480
we've seen students not being able to get jobs

642
00:27:09,480 --> 00:27:10,880
in fields that are...

643
00:27:11,910 --> 00:27:14,407
You think, especially in this area, in the Bay Area, that,

644
00:27:14,407 --> 00:27:16,530
"Oh, yeah, there's so many jobs in programming,

645
00:27:16,530 --> 00:27:17,910
in computer science, et cetera,"

646
00:27:17,910 --> 00:27:19,770
but now they're having some issues getting those jobs

647
00:27:19,770 --> 00:27:21,810
because when they went to university,

648
00:27:21,810 --> 00:27:25,770
they were not trained on AI, whatever concept it is.

649
00:27:25,770 --> 00:27:27,570
And so I think that's where the community colleges

650
00:27:27,570 --> 00:27:28,710
are doing great.

651
00:27:28,710 --> 00:27:30,060
A lot of the community colleges

652
00:27:30,060 --> 00:27:34,740
are adopting up to what 15 districts now

653
00:27:34,740 --> 00:27:37,140
are adopting new AI courses

654
00:27:37,140 --> 00:27:39,180
and training for students in various areas,

655
00:27:39,180 --> 00:27:41,160
not just in computer science and technology

656
00:27:41,160 --> 00:27:44,435
and CIT, et cetera, but also in other ones.

657
00:27:44,435 --> 00:27:47,880
We have faculty in our college that from math to...

658
00:27:47,880 --> 00:27:49,350
One of my faculty members,

659
00:27:49,350 --> 00:27:52,260
from math to other disciplines

660
00:27:52,260 --> 00:27:53,640
that are really interested in embedding it

661
00:27:53,640 --> 00:27:58,413
into their courses, et cetera, in the teaching.

662
00:27:59,370 --> 00:28:00,600
I think one important thing

663
00:28:00,600 --> 00:28:01,920
that I always tell my faculty, though,

664
00:28:01,920 --> 00:28:05,730
and I tell everyone is just make sure that...

665
00:28:05,730 --> 00:28:06,930
That's blinking a lot. - I know.

666
00:28:06,930 --> 00:28:09,810
- Just make sure that everything's been done

667
00:28:09,810 --> 00:28:11,730
with ethics in mind.

668
00:28:11,730 --> 00:28:13,200
Ethics is very important.

669
00:28:13,200 --> 00:28:16,570
We have to make sure that we're not assigning personhood

670
00:28:17,680 --> 00:28:19,290
to the AI system, we're not calling it he,

671
00:28:19,290 --> 00:28:22,110
or we're making sure that it's the person using it

672
00:28:22,110 --> 00:28:24,360
that is doing that is not the computer system

673
00:28:24,360 --> 00:28:26,040
that told them the answer.

674
00:28:26,040 --> 00:28:27,300
No, you did that answer.

675
00:28:27,300 --> 00:28:29,250
So there has to be some accountability as well

676
00:28:29,250 --> 00:28:30,570
for work done.

677
00:28:30,570 --> 00:28:33,510
There has to be some guidance as well

678
00:28:33,510 --> 00:28:35,743
as part of that ethics and training.

679
00:28:35,743 --> 00:28:37,950
There have to be guidance from us,

680
00:28:37,950 --> 00:28:40,260
from our faculty members, and I count myself in that,

681
00:28:40,260 --> 00:28:41,670
to the students on how to use it,

682
00:28:41,670 --> 00:28:42,900
what's acceptable, et cetera.

683
00:28:42,900 --> 00:28:44,850
And how to do it so that everyone benefits,

684
00:28:44,850 --> 00:28:46,023
not just those who can.

685
00:28:47,670 --> 00:28:50,880
- If you have a student or a child

686
00:28:50,880 --> 00:28:55,680
who is anywhere from two years old to 25 years old

687
00:28:55,680 --> 00:28:59,400
or any of your students kind of in that same age category,

688
00:28:59,400 --> 00:29:00,868
do any of you have the same feelings

689
00:29:00,868 --> 00:29:03,900
of you have to now learn a whole new vocabulary

690
00:29:03,900 --> 00:29:06,900
to stay in tune with what's happening around you?

691
00:29:06,900 --> 00:29:07,980
I know I feel that way,

692
00:29:07,980 --> 00:29:09,337
there's a lot of words where I'm like,

693
00:29:09,337 --> 00:29:10,800
"What? That doesn't mean that."

694
00:29:10,800 --> 00:29:14,220
And so I think we all have those moments in our life.

695
00:29:14,220 --> 00:29:16,410
With this, I think Dreamscape Learn

696
00:29:16,410 --> 00:29:20,430
is one of those where we have to relate to our students,

697
00:29:20,430 --> 00:29:21,870
we have to adapt to our students,

698
00:29:21,870 --> 00:29:24,220
we have to consider what they're going through.

699
00:29:25,080 --> 00:29:28,110
Two important facts that stick out to me

700
00:29:28,110 --> 00:29:29,970
when I present this to colleagues

701
00:29:29,970 --> 00:29:34,970
is 85% of our incoming students in 2024 played video games.

702
00:29:35,910 --> 00:29:39,960
Dreamscape Learn lends to that environment,

703
00:29:39,960 --> 00:29:42,030
and so it is kind of that video game design.

704
00:29:42,030 --> 00:29:45,150
So therefore, it is connecting with a lot of our students.

705
00:29:45,150 --> 00:29:47,370
Therefore, students have higher engagement.

706
00:29:47,370 --> 00:29:49,170
Therefore, students learn more

707
00:29:49,170 --> 00:29:51,630
because they're more engaged in their process.

708
00:29:51,630 --> 00:29:54,180
They're more connected to their learning experience.

709
00:29:54,180 --> 00:29:55,830
So I think we have to consider that.

710
00:29:55,830 --> 00:29:56,970
The other thing to consider

711
00:29:56,970 --> 00:29:58,290
that I always like to tell colleagues

712
00:29:58,290 --> 00:30:00,487
is for every dollar made in the movie industry,

713
00:30:00,487 --> 00:30:03,240
$7 is made in the video game industry.

714
00:30:03,240 --> 00:30:06,060
So for a lot of us, we hear box-office numbers,

715
00:30:06,060 --> 00:30:08,497
where "Titanic" made, say, whatever,

716
00:30:08,497 --> 00:30:11,760
$40 million over the weekend at the box office.

717
00:30:11,760 --> 00:30:13,590
For us, that was common vocabulary

718
00:30:13,590 --> 00:30:15,120
that we used to hear these numbers,

719
00:30:15,120 --> 00:30:18,330
and that's how we judged how much money that movie made.

720
00:30:18,330 --> 00:30:20,970
In the video game industry, it's seven times more.

721
00:30:20,970 --> 00:30:22,860
And so just recognizing the power,

722
00:30:22,860 --> 00:30:24,600
when we hear from Stewart Knox

723
00:30:24,600 --> 00:30:26,490
talking about future-ready students

724
00:30:26,490 --> 00:30:28,350
and the jobs that are gonna be available,

725
00:30:28,350 --> 00:30:30,180
we have to start thinking about those.

726
00:30:30,180 --> 00:30:32,340
And Dreamscape Learn, I think, lends to that.

727
00:30:32,340 --> 00:30:34,980
It's that video game design, it's that animation.

728
00:30:34,980 --> 00:30:37,200
There's a lot to be had there and to unpack.

729
00:30:37,200 --> 00:30:38,250
And so as colleagues,

730
00:30:38,250 --> 00:30:39,540
I think we have to work with each other

731
00:30:39,540 --> 00:30:40,860
to find those connection points.

732
00:30:40,860 --> 00:30:41,990
I'm not saying Dreamscape Learn

733
00:30:41,990 --> 00:30:44,370
is the only avenue to connect with students

734
00:30:44,370 --> 00:30:46,200
and to relate to students,

735
00:30:46,200 --> 00:30:48,120
but I do think it is one hook,

736
00:30:48,120 --> 00:30:50,760
that is a possibility for a lot of our colleagues,

737
00:30:50,760 --> 00:30:52,503
to be able to engage with faculty.

738
00:30:55,500 --> 00:30:59,220
- Okay, just don't wanna repeat what's already been said.

739
00:30:59,220 --> 00:31:02,430
One thing that working with my faculty colleagues,

740
00:31:02,430 --> 00:31:05,583
it's keeping that human being in the equation.

741
00:31:06,990 --> 00:31:09,360
It's great, like the AI tools in Canvas

742
00:31:09,360 --> 00:31:11,580
that are really shortcuts for grading.

743
00:31:11,580 --> 00:31:14,130
One feedback we had was, for example,

744
00:31:14,130 --> 00:31:15,720
the discussion summaries.

745
00:31:15,720 --> 00:31:17,970
That really is helpful for grading.

746
00:31:17,970 --> 00:31:22,740
It's really not so helpful for responding to the students.

747
00:31:22,740 --> 00:31:24,210
What I found when I was using it

748
00:31:24,210 --> 00:31:26,640
with the course I was teaching at the time

749
00:31:26,640 --> 00:31:30,540
was that I tried just using the summaries, right?

750
00:31:30,540 --> 00:31:33,780
And it was telling me basically what the students said,

751
00:31:33,780 --> 00:31:34,710
but what I missed

752
00:31:34,710 --> 00:31:37,350
was that they were asking me questions in there somewhere.

753
00:31:37,350 --> 00:31:39,937
And when I actually went to the discussion post, it's like,

754
00:31:39,937 --> 00:31:42,090
"I'm not really clear on this concept,"

755
00:31:42,090 --> 00:31:45,360
and I'm like, "Oh, I need to answer them."

756
00:31:45,360 --> 00:31:48,090
Otherwise, we just have the AI, like, grading the AI,

757
00:31:48,090 --> 00:31:50,377
which kind of becomes pointless, right?

758
00:31:50,377 --> 00:31:51,750
(audience laughing)

759
00:31:51,750 --> 00:31:52,583
Okay.

760
00:31:54,630 --> 00:31:59,310
- So, what I would suggest for faculty is simply this.

761
00:31:59,310 --> 00:32:00,573
Start small.

762
00:32:02,730 --> 00:32:05,070
Find a pain point that you have with your course.

763
00:32:05,070 --> 00:32:08,820
Say it's due dates, say it's syllabus,

764
00:32:08,820 --> 00:32:11,700
say it's APA, or any of those things.

765
00:32:11,700 --> 00:32:13,140
Find your pain point.

766
00:32:13,140 --> 00:32:14,220
And actually, one of the ways

767
00:32:14,220 --> 00:32:16,050
that you can find those pain points

768
00:32:16,050 --> 00:32:18,870
is to put a bot in there that they start asking questions.

769
00:32:18,870 --> 00:32:20,550
And on a weekly basis,

770
00:32:20,550 --> 00:32:22,770
you take a look at the analytics and find out:

771
00:32:22,770 --> 00:32:24,390
What is the most common question?

772
00:32:24,390 --> 00:32:26,670
Where is the confusion coming from?

773
00:32:26,670 --> 00:32:30,090
And then start focusing on those pain points.

774
00:32:30,090 --> 00:32:34,560
We're not trying to AIify the entire course,

775
00:32:34,560 --> 00:32:35,610
that's not the intent.

776
00:32:35,610 --> 00:32:40,610
The intent is to mitigate on the student side

777
00:32:41,130 --> 00:32:43,920
and to make your time more valuable

778
00:32:43,920 --> 00:32:46,710
into what you need to do as instruction,

779
00:32:46,710 --> 00:32:49,590
not answering the email five different times

780
00:32:49,590 --> 00:32:51,540
about a due date, okay?

781
00:32:51,540 --> 00:32:55,350
So start off small, start off with what you know,

782
00:32:55,350 --> 00:32:57,540
and then you can move on from that point.

783
00:32:57,540 --> 00:33:01,083
And so, that's what I would tell colleagues.

784
00:33:05,010 --> 00:33:09,150
- All right, so now we're gonna address another topic

785
00:33:09,150 --> 00:33:12,690
that we hear often, which is...

786
00:33:12,690 --> 00:33:15,570
We know that there are concerns about academic integrity

787
00:33:15,570 --> 00:33:18,810
and the teaching and learning context when it comes to AI.

788
00:33:18,810 --> 00:33:19,830
At the same time,

789
00:33:19,830 --> 00:33:23,070
we heard a clarion call from the chancellor this morning

790
00:33:23,070 --> 00:33:24,690
that we need to prepare our students

791
00:33:24,690 --> 00:33:26,760
