00:35:41,940 --> 00:35:44,100
and students were, all of a sudden, using it,

848
00:35:44,100 --> 00:35:45,960
and we're all using it,

849
00:35:45,960 --> 00:35:48,900
and then the whole world is talking, and we are here now.

850
00:35:48,900 --> 00:35:50,340
And it feels like this was yesterday.

851
00:35:50,340 --> 00:35:52,440
Sometimes, it feels like we just started this conversation

852
00:35:52,440 --> 00:35:54,030
a couple months ago.

853
00:35:54,030 --> 00:35:56,280
But I think it's part of that what we have to do

854
00:35:56,280 --> 00:35:57,840
is make sure we understand it ourselves.

855
00:35:57,840 --> 00:36:00,930
So we have to start there because it's not going anywhere.

856
00:36:00,930 --> 00:36:03,150
And it's now about:

857
00:36:03,150 --> 00:36:07,050
How do we, just like computers when they came around

858
00:36:07,050 --> 00:36:09,090
and we didn't go to the library anymore,

859
00:36:09,090 --> 00:36:10,950
we did everything on the computer and so on,

860
00:36:10,950 --> 00:36:12,810
but people were still, "No, you have to do the library,

861
00:36:12,810 --> 00:36:14,430
not the computer, et cetera, et cetera."

862
00:36:14,430 --> 00:36:16,320
It's about: How do we change our mindset

863
00:36:16,320 --> 00:36:17,310
in the way we teach?

864
00:36:17,310 --> 00:36:19,410
Teaching is an ever-evolving process.

865
00:36:19,410 --> 00:36:21,120
I think in the last session somebody was saying

866
00:36:21,120 --> 00:36:24,237
about teaching cybersecurity like it was in 2012,

867
00:36:24,237 --> 00:36:26,970
and that shouldn't work anymore and shouldn't.

868
00:36:26,970 --> 00:36:27,810
So it's kind of like that, right?

869
00:36:27,810 --> 00:36:29,130
Teaching changes with times,

870
00:36:29,130 --> 00:36:31,800
and it changes with technology, and it's about:

871
00:36:31,800 --> 00:36:34,091
How do we implement the new

872
00:36:34,091 --> 00:36:36,270
and how do we use it to help the students?

873
00:36:36,270 --> 00:36:40,413
Ultimately, as I said earlier, survey your students.

874
00:36:42,000 --> 00:36:42,930
What are they looking for?

875
00:36:42,930 --> 00:36:44,730
What are they thinking?

876
00:36:44,730 --> 00:36:47,220
Because they're the ones who are going to go out there

877
00:36:47,220 --> 00:36:48,480
after they leave your class,

878
00:36:48,480 --> 00:36:52,080
after they leave our colleges and universities and get jobs.

879
00:36:52,080 --> 00:36:53,850
And are they able to get those jobs?

880
00:36:53,850 --> 00:36:56,430
And how do we support that now, which is our mission?

881
00:36:56,430 --> 00:36:58,110
How do we support those students

882
00:36:58,110 --> 00:37:02,010
to get the jobs which are going to use AI no matter what?

883
00:37:02,010 --> 00:37:04,770
And how do we do it in a meaningful way?

884
00:37:04,770 --> 00:37:05,603
Because it is our job to do it

885
00:37:05,603 --> 00:37:08,297
in a way that really leads somewhere, not just as...

886
00:37:08,297 --> 00:37:10,800
'cause students are teaching themselves.

887
00:37:10,800 --> 00:37:13,290
They're teaching each other, they're learning online,

888
00:37:13,290 --> 00:37:15,090
and that's how they're getting through.

889
00:37:15,090 --> 00:37:16,710
And so we have an opportunity here.

890
00:37:16,710 --> 00:37:20,130
This is not a bad thing, an opportunity to capture,

891
00:37:20,130 --> 00:37:24,240
to capture a generation that needs to be trained on AI

892
00:37:24,240 --> 00:37:27,190
and those who already kind of use it and need to get there.

893
00:37:29,520 --> 00:37:33,720
- So when we're talking about academic integrity,

894
00:37:33,720 --> 00:37:35,520
we have to set boundaries.

895
00:37:35,520 --> 00:37:37,530
We know that they're gonna use it.

896
00:37:37,530 --> 00:37:38,630
And what we need to do

897
00:37:38,630 --> 00:37:42,210
is we need to set those boundaries right off the bat

